<h2>What we look for</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Bachelor's or master's in Engineering, Computer Science, or Applied Physics.</li>
  <li>Strong fundamentals in solid mechanics, creativity, and motivation for research.</li>
  <li>Programming proficiency (Python), and experience with FEM/FEA (Abaqus, ANSYS, COMSOL), CAD/AM, and/or robotics.</li>
  <li>Clear, proactive communication, and the ability to work both independently and in a team.</li>
  <li>Prior research experience or publications are an advantage but not required.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What we offer</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Training in experimental mechanics, advanced manufacturing, and architected metamaterials, spanning experiment, computation, and theory.</li>
  <li>Access to the department's and the College's research facilities, and to collaborators across the College.</li>
  <li>Opportunities for conference travel, publication, and cross-disciplinary projects.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to apply</h2>
<p>There are two application routes. Each year the Department of Aeronautics runs a competitive round of fully funded PhD Studentships, covering fees and a tax-free stipend, with applications typically opening in the autumn and closing in early January. Outside that round, you can apply at any time through Imperial's online application system, naming your proposed supervisor and any funding you are seeking.</p>
<p>In both cases, email <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">Dr. Chen</a> first with a CV and a short statement of your research interests and how they relate to the group's work.</p>
